{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966815,"nonce":"Ngm2NB/WSVEXr8ZHR+WYBL1wPJM=","title":"Slint-inspired renderer rework — adoption candidates","message":"Internal study of Slint (https://slint.dev, source at slint-ui/slint) prompted by issue #28 OP comparing Slint's idle-CPU and animation perf to Blinc's. The recurring symptom — one continuously animating element on screen pinning the redraw chain at full vsync — is architecturally addressable.\n\n**Why:** OP's comparison is fair on the \"single small element animating\" case. Slint's per-item damage tracking redraws only the changed rect; Blinc redraws the whole window. After our recent visibility-gate fix train (`333bc4e5` … `74dfdba2`), idle-CPU is on par; the gap is now in active-animation CPU, and adopting Slint's dirty-region machinery would close it.\n\n**How to apply:** treat as a phased follow-up to the renderer.rs split refactor (also tracked in this memory directory if extracted). Don't start any of these phases on the current 13K-LOC `renderer.rs` — the file split needs to land first or every change here will hit merge pain.\n\n\n\nSlint's `has_active_animations()` is per-window. Blinc's `frame_dirty` is per `WindowState`, but our global flags (`visible_anim_for_wake`, `STATEFUL_ANIMATIONS` registry) need a quick audit to confirm animations on window A don't keep window B's chain hot. Audit + possibly small fix.\n\n\nThe flagship change. Slint's [`internal/core/partial_renderer.rs`](https://github.com/slint-ui/slint/blob/master/internal/core/partial_renderer.rs) walks the item tree once, compares each item's current bounding rect against a cached one, asks a per-item `PropertyTracker` if writes happened, and unions the changed rects into a `DirtyRegion`. Cap at 3 rectangles, overflow → union.\n\nFor Blinc:\n- Bounding-rect cache on `RenderTree` keyed by `LayoutNodeId` (we have layout bounds — close).\n- A `PropertyTracker`-equivalent per node — the hard part. Slint gets it free from their compiled property system. We'd intercept `update_render_props` mutation sites + animation tick writes.\n- `DirtyRegion` type + scissor plumbing into the GPU pipeline (`render_with_clear` accepts an optional rect-list clamp).\n- Cache invalidation on subtree rebuild, scroll, layer effects. Blur/shadow extends the dirty rect by their kernel radius — easy to forget.\n\nLands in `crates/blinc_layout/src/renderer/painter.rs` + a new `crates/blinc_layout/src/renderer/damage.rs` once the file split is done.\n\n\nSlint's three buffer policies: `NewBuffer` (always full), `ReusedBuffer` (paint only the new dirty region), `SwappedBuffers` (union the last N frames' regions to cover what the current back buffer is missing — keeps `dirty_region_history: [DirtyRegion; 3]`). Pairs with Phase 2.\n\nThe `SwappedBuffers` case is what wgpu surfaces actually look like — when wgpu hands us texture N+1, the contents are texture N-2. Without unioning, partial redraws tear.\n\n\nSlint's reactive system: animated property writes notify registered trackers; trackers feed the dirty-rect computation directly. We have `State\u003cT\u003e` signals + `register_stateful_animation`; could extend State to flag a render-relevant dirty bit when written from an animation tick path, intersected with `painted_node_ids`. Less general than Slint's tracker (theirs covers any property; ours covers State writes), but covers the spinner case directly.\n\n\nSlint's compiled-UI model: components + items + properties + trackers in a single contiguous allocation. We won't match the compile-time aspect, but co-locating `RenderProps` + `cached_bounds` + `PropertyTracker` per node into a single SoA layout the paint walker iterates linearly is achievable. Mid-scale refactor in `painter.rs`.\n\n\n- **Compiled UI model (`.slint` → Rust/C++).** Eliminates per-frame tree construction, but Blinc's identity is runtime construction (Div builders, Stateful, hot data-driven UI). Trading that away makes a different framework.\n- **Skia / FemtoVG delegation.** Slint inherits all of Skia's optimisations for free + ships a 20 MB binary. We have custom SDF shadows, 3D SDFs, glass blur (Kawase) — Skia doesn't do those. Wrong tradeoff.\n- **Software renderer (`no_std`, scanline).** Only matters for MCU targets. Out of scope.\n\n\n- Damage tracking: [`internal/core/partial_renderer.rs`](https://github.com/slint-ui/slint/blob/master/internal/core/partial_renderer.rs)\n- Event-loop gating: [`internal/backends/winit/event_loop.rs`](https://github.com/slint-ui/slint/blob/master/internal/backends/winit/event_loop.rs)\n- Software renderer scene: [`internal/renderers/software/scene.rs`](https://github.com/slint-ui/slint/blob/master/internal/renderers/software/scene.rs)\n- PR adding partial-renderer geometry/render split: [#9467](https://github.com/slint-ui/slint/pull/9467)\n- Idle-CPU regression they hit: [#5780](https://github.com/slint-ui/slint/issues/5780) (instructive — even Slint occasionally regresses idle CPU; the tracker isn't a silver bullet)\n\n\n`renderer.rs` is split into submodules under `crates/blinc_layout/src/renderer/`. Phase 2's edits would otherwise hit a 13K-LOC file with merge conflicts on every concurrent change. Refactor-first is mandatory.","files":null}]}